    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d488">

                <pb facs="0549" n="537" sameAs="frus:frus1943v05/pg537"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">824.50/294: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Bolwia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Boal</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">La
                                Paz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-12-23T20:00:00-04:00">December 23, 1943—8
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 24—3:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2013. For Assistant Secretary Berle. Please telegraph for my own information what
                    the situation is with regard to Bolivian funds and Government monetary reserves
                    in the United States. Are they <pb facs="0550" n="538" xml:id="pg538"/> blocked?
                    Development Corporation has about 3 million dollars in the Banco Central which
                    comes from former Bolivian Government deposit. You may wish to see whether
                    Export-Import Bank desires to instruct its directors in La Paz including
                        Egger<anchor n="14" xml:id="d488-f1"/> against any withdrawals either in
                    Bolivia or in the United States in excess of regular running expenses. While
                        Inslee<anchor n="15" xml:id="d488-f2"/> has some fears regarding this as a
                    precedent for the Bolivian side both Egger and Crespo<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d488-f3"/> feel this would be very desirable if done
                    immediately.</p>
                <p>In view of Crespo’s arrest and later release today “because his cooperation would
                    be needed” Egger and I fear the Junta might seek to put pressure on the
                    directors to use their present powers by threatening the welfare Crespo and his
                    family.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Boal</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d488-f1" n="14" xml:id="d488-f1-content">
                <p>Rowland A. Egger, Manager, Bolivian Development Corporation.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d488-f2" n="15" xml:id="d488-f2-content">
                <p>Joseph A. Inslee, Representative of the Export-Import Bank.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d488-f3" n="16" xml:id="d488-f3-content">
                <p>Alberto Crespo Gutierrez, President of the Bolivian Development Corporation and
                    former Minister of National Economy.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
